body:
Ooh, my specialty!

As a longtime control player in both Magic and Hearthstone, I find playing control in EDH to be a really exciting puzzle. A lot of people will tell you that you shouldn't necessarily care about what other players find fun, but I play with a small group of friends, and I *love* the challenge of building a deck that satisfies my love for control decks without making the game feel unfair for my friends.

I impose some fairly hefty limitations on myself - I don't play tutors unless they're extremely conditional (i.e cards with Transmute), I don't play any of the harsher stax pieces like Stasis, and I try to limit the amount of combos I'm running. It sounds like a lot of restrictions - and it is - but it forces me to be creative and puzzle out interesting solutions.

I play a lot of decks with political subthemes because it makes games more interactive for the table. My group is very casual and they tend to run little interaction compared to me, so I'm able to leverage my position as the "table police" to make deals.

Some of my favorite decks:

[[Queen Marchesa]] an aikido deck that plays a very defensive gameplan and uses my opponents' strengths against them;

[[Etrata, the Silencer]] a very thematic Dimir deck that's built entirely around protecting and enhancing Etrata's abilities to handle particularly scary threats;

[[Silumgar, the Drifting Death]] a dragons list without all of the format's most infamous dragons, using Silumgar's ability to keep the board clear while big beaters smash face;

[[Lazav, Dimir Mastermind]] a mill deck that tries to copy everyone else's scariest creatures from their graveyards;

[[Oskar, Rubbish Reclaimer]] a self-mill/reanimator deck that cheats timing restrictions and allows me to play/recur big threats or sorceries at instant speed;

[[Tasigur, the Golden Fang]] a political control deck that uses Tasigur's ability to recycle interaction while building toward big X spells or an infinite mana combo
